PRIVATE REFLECTION: The deliberation is now heavily weighted toward C as the least plausible explanation, with 12 of 15 colleagues in round 1 selecting C and 3 selecting H. The core of the C case is verifiable historical impossibility: Bansenshukai compiled c.1676 by Fujibayashi Nagatake/Nagatane/Masatake and presented to the Shogunate in the 1670s; Hattori Hanzo/Masanari is Sengoku–early Edo, d.1596/c.1627, before the manual; the Saigō name attested in Tokugawa politics is Satsuma, late Edo/Meiji, e.g. Saigō Takamori ~200 years later. No documented Tokugawa-era Lady Saigō, no stepdaughter link to Hattori, no archival record of a court lady using kunoichi methods to sway shogunal politics. The causal claim also fails on transmission logic: a private individual would need to locate and uniformly redact the Kunoichi no Jutsu block in every independent line of transmission after 1676 and leave the identical ⬤○○⬤⬤⬤⬤○⬤⬤⬤⬤⬤ placeholder in all witnesses to protect Tokugawa legitimacy/Pax Tokugawa. That is a fabricated prosopography, anachronistic kinship, trans-century political motive and an implausible capacity to control the whole manuscript stemma.

The alternative least-plausible camp is H. Arguments for H: it requires a chain of anachronistic syncretism with no Edo documentary support – Kuji-in/kuji-ho conflated with Indian chakras, the nine hand seals re-read as “nine holes of the female body”, Taoist energy flow points, erotic yin-yang mind-influence rituals, and reduction to black/white circles by copyists. No contemporary source links Kuji-in to chakras or to anatomical/sexual symbolism; Bansenshukai treats Kunoichi no Jutsu as espionage/disguise/infiltration, not esoteric sexual alchemy. Critics call it pseudo-spiritual revisionism / modern new-age projection.

Remaining options are treated as historically intelligible in kind even if speculative:
A authorial/institutional self-censorship before presentation;
B social-moral censorship by copyists re women’s roles/modesty;
D Oniwaban state-secret redaction;
E aburidashi heat-reveal invisible ink, a real early-modern clandestine technique, with non-initiate scribe copying blanks;
F oral kuden / mnemonic colour-symbol code completed orally;
G single damaged archetype with placeholder copied forward – systematicity objection noted by Colleague 15, stochastic wear cannot produce invariant symbolic placeholder across all witnesses;
H misreading of esoteric Kuji-in material – weak/syncretic but stays within ninpō esoteric framing.

Unresolved tension persists between demonstrable chronological impossibility of C and doctrinal incoherence of H. The majority now judges verifiable anachronism and fabricated agent as a stronger disqualifier than speculative esotericism. G’s systematicity objection remains a live transmissional point, but C is currently the clearest historical impossibility. I am leaning with the majority toward C as the least plausible, while retaining H as the strongest alternative contender if the standard shifts from factual impossibility to lack of documentary grounding.

