public_text.13:
I’m staying with H as the least plausible of the set, and I think the stemma point actually strengthens that conclusion once we separate mechanism from nominal specificity.

The ⬤○○⬤⬤⬤⬤○⬤⬤⬤⬤ pattern is an invariant lacuna, so we need a single normalising event in the archetype that is then faithfully copied. A, B, D, E, F, G and a de-personalised reading of C all satisfy that shape with a mechanism that stays inside documented Tokugawa manuscript culture: authorial/institutional self-censorship before presentation to the Shogun; social/moral censorship by copyists around women’s roles and modesty; Oniwaban state-secret redaction; aburidashi heat-reveal writing transcribed as blanks by a non-initiate; a kuden/oral mnemonic colour-symbol schematic reproduced as a placeholder; a single damaged archetype with a conventional scribal placeholder propagated downstream. Those are all speculative in detail, but they are historically intelligible kinds of censorship, secrecy, pedagogy and scribal convention.

C is being discussed now almost entirely as a nominal specificity problem. Yes, ‘Lady Saigō, stepdaughter to Hattori, key Tokugawa figure’ is a thin and anachronistic gloss on names and dates when read literally. Shinobi historiography is messy; court ladies, concubines and female intelligence assets are essentially absent from official registers and names, kinships and dates are routinely conflated in later commentaries and glosses. Stripped of the name, the core move is a Tokugawa-era suppression/redaction of a sensitive kunoichi block to protect shogunal legitimacy/Pax Tokugawa. That is not a novel class of explanation, it is the personalised narrative retelling of exactly the institutional secrecy logic that underwrites A, B and D. The objections to C in the last round are objections to attestation and gloss, not to mechanism. A de-personalised institutional reading of C is historically intelligible as state/political redaction and it can produce the uniform lacuna via a single archetypal redaction.

H is different. It does not stay inside censorship, secrecy, pedagogy or scribal transmission. It requires a wholesale doctrinal importation with no Edo anchor:

* Kuji-in/kujiho in the Bansenshukai is presented as a Shingon/Onmyōdō-derived set of mystic seals and mnemonics, not as a chakra system;
* there is no pre-modern Japanese source linking Kujiho to Indian chakras;
* ‘nine holes of the female body’ as a technical Taoist hui cultivation mapping is modern fetish slang, not Edo technical vocabulary;
* the Bansenshukai frames Kunoichi no Jutsu in conventional espionage terms — disguise, infiltration, intelligence gathering — not occult Taoist erotic alchemy for mind influence;
* and the proposed degradation pathway — mystic seals → black/white circles → chakra anatomy → Taoist sexual ritual → invariant dot placeholder copied perfectly in all witnesses while meaning is lost — has no manuscript evidence.

A to G, and a de-personalised institutional reading of C, stay within the known world of Tokugawa manuscript control and esoteric pedagogy. H alone requires a fabricated doctrine: a modern new-age projection of chakras, Taoist sexual alchemy and anatomical ‘nine holes’ back onto 1676, and then an improbable uniform loss of meaning. That is the only option that invents a whole new doctrinal system for the period rather than invoking a documented practice of censorship, secrecy or transmission loss. That makes H the least historically grounded explanation for why every surviving transcription shows the same systematic blank in the same place.

